\title{Projective Completion from Planar Ternary Coordinates:\\Incidence, Enumeration, and the Algebraic Obstruction of Nonassociativity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 26, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We isolate the minimal unique-solvability assumptions needed to construct a projective incidence structure from coordinates. A planar ternary coordinate system consists of a set $A$ and an operation $T:A^3\to A$ satisfying three axioms: unique solution for the intercept in $T(x,m,b)=y$; unique slope-intercept pair through two affine points with distinct first coordinates; and unique affine intersection for two ordinary lines of distinct slopes. From these data we construct affine points, ideal points, ordinary lines, vertical lines, and a line at infinity. We prove that every two distinct points determine a unique line and every two distinct lines meet in a unique point. If $|A|=q$, both the point and line sets have cardinality $q^2+q+1$. No associativity or distributivity hypothesis is used. We also characterize associativity of an arbitrary binary multiplication by its left nucleus. The results provide an incidence-theoretic foundation for nonclassical coordinatization while separating what follows from solvability alone from what is required to prove failure of Desargues' theorem or compute collineation groups.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Projective geometry completes affine geometry by adjoining a point for every direction and a line containing all such points. Over a field $F$, an affine line of finite slope has equation $y=xm+b$, vertical lines have equations $x=a$, and parallel lines meet at the ideal point corresponding to their direction. The standard construction may appear to depend on all the algebra of $F$. Its fundamental incidence properties use much less.

The crucial steps are solution operations. One must solve uniquely for an intercept through an affine point at a prescribed slope; solve uniquely for slope and intercept through two points whose first coordinates differ; and solve uniquely for the intersection of two lines whose slopes differ. These requirements can be imposed on one ternary operation $T(x,m,b)$ without splitting it into multiplication and addition.

A motivating theme is non-Desarguesian geometry, but the scope must be precise. Non-Desarguesian planes do not exist at every prime-power order: the plane of order $2$, for example, is unique and Desarguesian. The results below instead establish the general mechanism through which a suitable coordinate system generates projective incidence. Proving that a particular plane is non-Desarguesian requires a concrete coordinate algebra and an additional theorem relating Desargues configurations to its algebraic laws.

\section{Planar ternary coordinates}

\begin{definition}[Planar ternary coordinate system]
Let $A$ be a set. A planar ternary coordinate system on $A$ is an operation
\[
T:A\times A\times A\longrightarrow A
\]
satisfying the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For every $x,m,y\in A$, exactly one $b\in A$ satisfies $T(x,m,b)=y$.
\item If $x_1\ne x_2$, then for every $y_1,y_2\in A$, exactly one $(m,b)\in A^2$ satisfies
\[
T(x_1,m,b)=y_1,\qquad T(x_2,m,b)=y_2.
\]
\item If $m_1\ne m_2$, then for every $b_1,b_2\in A$, exactly one $(x,y)\in A^2$ satisfies
\[
y=T(x,m_1,b_1),\qquad y=T(x,m_2,b_2).
\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The letters suggest first coordinate, slope, ordinate, and intercept. There are no assumed constants, binary operations, normalization laws, distributive laws, or associativity laws.

\begin{example}[Field coordinates]
For a field $F$, let $T(x,m,b)=xm+b$. The intercept is obtained by subtraction. For $x_1\ne x_2$, the unique line parameters are
\[
m=(y_2-y_1)(x_2-x_1)^{-1},\qquad b=y_1-x_1m.
\]
For $m_1\ne m_2$, the unique intersection has
\[
x=(b_2-b_1)(m_1-m_2)^{-1},\qquad y=xm_1+b_1.
\]
Thus field coordinates satisfy all three axioms.
\end{example}

\section{The projective completion}

\begin{definition}[Points and lines]
The point set is the disjoint union
\[
\mathcal P=A^2\sqcup A\sqcup\{I(\infty)\}.
\]
Its elements are affine points $P(x,y)$, finite-slope ideal points $I(m)$, and one vertical ideal point $I(\infty)$. The line set is
\[
\mathcal L=A^2\sqcup A\sqcup\{L(\infty)\}.
\]
Its elements are ordinary lines $L(m,b)$, vertical lines $V(a)$, and the line at infinity $L(\infty)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Incidence]
Incidence is defined by these rules:
\begin{itemize}
\item $P(x,y)$ lies on $L(m,b)$ iff $y=T(x,m,b)$;
\item $P(x,y)$ lies on $V(a)$ iff $x=a$;
\item no affine point lies on $L(\infty)$;
\item $I(m)$ lies on $L(n,b)$ iff $m=n$;
\item $I(m)$ lies on no vertical line and every $I(m)$ lies on $L(\infty)$;
\item $I(\infty)$ lies on every vertical line and on $L(\infty)$, but on no ordinary line.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Thus parallel ordinary lines share the ideal point indexed by their slope, while all vertical lines share the separate point $I(\infty)$.

\section{Unique joining lines}

\begin{theorem}[Unique Joining-Line Theorem]
In the completion of a planar ternary coordinate system, every two distinct points are incident with exactly one common line.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let the points be distinct. If they are affine, write them as $P(x_1,y_1)$ and $P(x_2,y_2)$. When $x_1=x_2$, their common line is $V(x_1)$. No ordinary line can contain both, because the unique-intercept axiom at fixed $x_1$ and fixed slope would force $y_1=y_2$; no other vertical line works. When $x_1\ne x_2$, the second solvability axiom supplies the unique $(m,b)$ for $L(m,b)$. A vertical line cannot contain both, and the line at infinity contains neither.

For $P(x,y)$ and $I(m)$, an ordinary common line must have slope $m$, and the unique-intercept axiom supplies exactly one $b$ with $T(x,m,b)=y$. Vertical lines do not contain $I(m)$ and the line at infinity does not contain $P(x,y)$. For $P(x,y)$ and $I(\infty)$, the unique common line is $V(x)$.

Finally, two distinct ideal points share $L(\infty)$. Two finite-slope ideal points cannot share an ordinary line unless their slopes coincide, and neither lies on a vertical line. A finite-slope ideal point and $I(\infty)$ share neither an ordinary nor a vertical line. Hence the line at infinity is unique in every ideal-point case.
\end{proof}

\section{Unique intersections}

\begin{theorem}[Unique Intersection Theorem]
In the completion of a planar ternary coordinate system, every two distinct lines are incident with exactly one common point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Consider first $L(m_1,b_1)$ and $L(m_2,b_2)$. If $m_1\ne m_2$, the third solvability axiom gives a unique affine point $P(x,y)$ satisfying both line equations. No ideal point lies on both because their slopes differ. If $m_1=m_2$, distinctness implies $b_1\ne b_2$. Both lines contain $I(m_1)$. A common affine point would give
\[
T(x,m_1,b_1)=y=T(x,m_1,b_2),
\]
and unique intercept would force $b_1=b_2$. Thus $I(m_1)$ is the unique intersection.

An ordinary line $L(m,b)$ and a vertical line $V(a)$ meet at
\[
P\bigl(a,T(a,m,b)\bigr).
\]
Any common affine point is forced to have these coordinates, and the lines have distinct ideal points. Two distinct vertical lines meet uniquely at $I(\infty)$, since no affine point has two distinct first coordinates.

Finally, $L(m,b)$ meets $L(\infty)$ exactly at $I(m)$, and $V(a)$ meets $L(\infty)$ exactly at $I(\infty)$. These cases exhaust all distinct line pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Projective unique incidence]
An incidence structure has projective unique incidence if every two distinct points lie on exactly one common line and every two distinct lines meet in exactly one common point.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Projective Completion Theorem]
Every planar ternary coordinate system has a canonical completion with projective unique incidence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use the point set, line set, and incidence relation above. The two required clauses are precisely the Unique Joining-Line and Unique Intersection Theorems.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Nondegeneracy]
Some definitions of projective plane additionally demand four points with no three collinear. The theorem states exactly the two uniqueness properties obtained uniformly from the three solvability axioms. For coordinate sets of suitable size, nondegeneracy may be established by selecting an explicit configuration.
\end{remark}

\section{Finite enumeration}
Assume $A$ is finite and $|A|=q$.

\begin{theorem}[Point Count]
The completion has exactly $q^2+q+1$ points.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The disjoint point classes contain $q^2$ affine points, $q$ finite-slope ideal points, and one vertical ideal point.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Line Count]
The completion has exactly $q^2+q+1$ lines.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
There are $q^2$ ordinary slope-intercept pairs, $q$ vertical lines, and one line at infinity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Point-line cardinality balance]
Every finite completion constructed above has equally many points and lines.
\end{corollary}

The balance follows directly from the two disjoint-union descriptions and does not require a duality or matrix-rank argument.

\section{The left nucleus and nonassociativity}
Let $Q$ be a set with an arbitrary binary operation $*:Q\times Q\to Q$.

\begin{definition}[Left nucleus]
The left nucleus of $*$ is
\[
N_\ell(*)=\{a\in Q: a*(b*c)=(a*b)*c\text{ for every }b,c\in Q\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Left-Nucleus Characterization]
The equality $N_\ell(*)=Q$ holds if and only if multiplication is associative on all triples:
\[
N_\ell(*)=Q\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\forall a,b,c\in Q,\ a*(b*c)=(a*b)*c.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If the nucleus is all of $Q$, then each $a$ satisfies its defining equality for arbitrary $b,c$, giving universal associativity. Conversely, universal associativity places every $a$ in the nucleus.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Proper-Nucleus Witness Theorem]
The left nucleus is proper if and only if there is an explicit associativity failure:
\[
N_\ell(*)\ne Q\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists a,b,c\in Q,\ a*(b*c)\ne(a*b)*c.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Negate the equivalence in the preceding theorem. More concretely, if $a\notin N_\ell(*)$, the definition supplies $b,c$ for which equality fails. Conversely, a failing triple excludes its leftmost entry from the nucleus.
\end{proof}

When $T(x,m,b)$ is presented as $x*m+b$, a proper left nucleus proves that $*$ is not field multiplication. It does not alone prove failure of Desargues' theorem. That conclusion needs a theorem translating a Desargues configuration into associativity under the relevant coordinatization hypotheses.

\section{Algorithms and computation}

\subsection{Completion enumeration}
Given a finite carrier $A$ and a table for $T$, list all $q^2$ affine points, $q$ ideal points, and the vertical ideal point. List all $q^2$ ordinary lines, $q$ vertical lines, and the line at infinity. Apply the incidence definition to each point-line pair. With $N=q^2+q+1$, constructing and storing the full incidence matrix costs $O(N^2)=O(q^4)$ time and space.

\subsection{Unique-incidence audit}
For each unordered pair of distinct points, count common incident lines and require one. For each unordered pair of distinct lines, count common incident points and require one. A transparent naive matrix implementation takes $O(N^3)=O(q^6)$ time, although the explicit formulas give faster structure-aware methods.

\subsection{Left-nucleus computation}
For each $a\in Q$, test all $(b,c)\in Q^2$ and retain $a$ precisely if $a*(b*c)=(a*b)*c$ always holds. This costs $O(|Q|^3)$ table lookups. The first failed comparison supplies a witness triple.

\section{Numerical example}
Let $A=\mathbb Z/q\mathbb Z$ for prime $q$, with
\[
T(x,m,b)=xm+b\pmod q.
\]
For $q=3$, the completion has $3^2+3+1=13$ points and lines. The affine points $P(0,1)$ and $P(2,2)$ determine the line $y=2x+1\pmod3$. Indeed,
\[
m=(2-1)(2-0)^{-1}=1\cdot2=2\pmod3,
\]
and $b=1$. This line meets $y=x+2$ at $P(1,0)$. The parallel lines $y=2x+1$ and $y=2x$ meet at $I(2)$, while the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ meet at $I(\infty)$.

For $q=2$, the construction has seven points and seven lines and gives the classical projective plane of order $2$. This is also an immediate warning against claiming a non-Desarguesian plane at every prime-power order.

\section{Applications}
The completion theorem permits weak coordinate systems to be studied by incidence rather than by guessed ring laws. A finite candidate table can be tested directly against the three solution axioms. Each failure has a geometric certificate: a missing or repeated intercept, a missing or repeated line through separated affine points, or a missing or repeated affine intersection for distinct slopes.

The finite completion also gives a square incidence matrix with $q^2+q+1$ rows and columns. Pairwise unique incidence connects these matrices with finite designs and coding theory. Uniform line sizes and the full parameters of a projective design require further counting statements, but the basic intersection architecture is already present.

The left-nucleus test complements this construction by diagnosing an algebraic defect in a proposed multiplication. For a quasifield candidate it can reveal nonassociativity with a concrete triple, after which one must still verify all division and ternary solvability laws.

\section{Scope and future work}
The construction is not a classification theorem and does not prove non-Desarguesian existence at every prime-power order. Nor does it imply that a collineation group is smaller than a corresponding projective linear group. A collineation is an incidence-preserving permutation, and a group comparison requires an explicit plane, an action, and an isomorphism or cardinality theorem. The quantity $q^2+q+1$ counts points and lines, not automorphisms.

Natural next steps are to impose the usual normalization laws and derive coordinates of the form $T(x,m,b)=x*m+b$; instantiate the theory with a fully established finite Hall quasifield; define Desargues configurations and prove the bridge from Desargues' theorem to associativity under appropriate translation-plane assumptions; compute collineation groups for concrete examples; and develop isotopy and isomorphism invariants for classification.

Hall triple systems should be treated separately from Hall quasifields unless a precise construction relates them. A shared historical name is not itself a mathematical connection. Likewise, a multiplication table is insufficient for a Hall-plane construction until every unique-solvability requirement has been established.

\section{Conclusion}
Three unique-solvability principles suffice for the essential projective completion of affine coordinates. They give exactly one line through each pair of distinct points and exactly one intersection for each pair of distinct lines without associativity or distributivity. For a finite coordinate set of size $q$, both incidence classes have size $q^2+q+1$.

The left nucleus gives a complementary algebraic criterion: it fills the carrier exactly when multiplication is associative, and its properness is exactly equivalent to an explicit failing triple. These results clarify both the power and the boundary of weak coordinatization: strong projective incidence follows from solvability, while non-Desarguesian examples, automorphism-group comparisons, and classification demand additional concrete structures and bridge theorems.

