# Explore Projective Completion

Begin with the geometric idea: ordinary affine coordinates gain one ideal point for each direction, and all ideal points lie on a horizon. For background, see [projective geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Projective_geometry) and [finite geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_geometry).

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The count $q^2+q+1$ comes from $q^2$ affine points, $q$ finite-slope ideal points, and one vertical ideal point. The same decomposition counts lines.

{{visualization:0}}

## From coordinates to incidence

Use the next widget to draw the unique line through two affine points over a small prime field.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the three solvability axioms</summary>
A ternary operation $T(x,m,b)$ must uniquely solve: (1) $T(x,m,b)=y$ for $b$; (2) the two equations through points with distinct first coordinates for $(m,b)$; and (3) two equations with distinct slopes for $(x,y)$. These are exactly the equation-solving steps used by projective completion.
</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

## Audit the whole finite structure

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the incidence proof architecture</summary>
Two affine points with equal first coordinate select a vertical line; with unequal first coordinates they invoke the second solvability law. Equal-slope ordinary lines meet ideally; unequal-slope lines invoke the third solvability law. Ordinary and vertical lines meet by direct evaluation, and all remaining parallelism is closed on the line at infinity.
</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

## Detect nonassociativity

The [nucleus of a nonassociative algebra](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nucleus_(algebra)) records elements that associate in a designated position. Explore the left nucleus here.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the proper-nucleus theorem</summary>
The left nucleus is the entire carrier exactly when every triple associates. Therefore it is proper exactly when some triple satisfies $a*(b*c)
e(a*b)*c$. This algebraic witness does not by itself prove a geometric failure of Desargues' theorem; an additional coordinatization bridge is required.
</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

## Mathematical boundary

The construction proves projective unique incidence and finite counts. It does not claim non-Desarguesian planes at every prime-power order, a universal statement contradicted by the plane of order $2$. It also does not compute collineation groups. Those conclusions require concrete examples and additional theorems. For context, explore [Desargues' theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desargues%27s_theorem) and [projective planes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Projective_plane).

